ASSISTANT 3. **Push detection**: `post-receive` hook in the bare repo. The hook script needs to trigger the checkout+agent pipeline. Options:
ASSISTANT    - Hook calls `systemctl start agent-run@<branch>.service` — needs the hook to run as root or a user with sudo rights. The git user could be allowed via sudoers or polkit to start the template service. Or the hook writes a file into a spool directory (e.g. `/var/lib/agent/inbox/`) and a systemd path unit triggers the run service. The spool approach decouples privileges: hook (running as git user) writes a small file; a systemd path unit on the spool dir starts the run service as root (or as agent user).
ASSISTANT    
ASSISTANT    I'll propose the spool + path unit approach: `post-receive` writes `/var/lib/agent/inbox/<branch>` (sanitized branch name) with the new sha; `systemd.path` on the inbox triggers `agent-run.service` (oneshot) which processes all pending files: for each, checkout branch into `/var/lib/agent/work/<branch>` and run the agent with that cwd.
